  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-4.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-4.$ The function reaches this value at both $(x,y)=(-3,-6)$ and $(x,y)=(-3,6).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14132654/assets/78348020535973cddc75c2d935c0ed2f0390b5330b4139105be80cb8f6e6032a.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
